EXPR(1)                          User Commands                         EXPR(1)

NAME
       expr - evaluate an expression and print the result

SYNOPSIS
       expr EXPRESSION
       expr OPTION

DESCRIPTION
       Evaluate EXPRESSION and write the result to standard output.

       EXPRESSION arrives as separate arguments, not as one string: the shell
       has already split it.  Every operator must therefore be separated by
       whitespace and, where the shell would otherwise claim it, quoted -- \*
       for multiplication, \| for or, \( \) for grouping.

       The exit status is inverted relative to the value.  It is 0 when the
       result is neither an empty string nor the number zero, and 1 when it
       is, which is what makes expr usable directly in an if.

OPTIONS
       --help display this help and exit

       --version
              output version information and exit

OPERATORS
       Listed from loosest to tightest binding.  Operators of equal
       precedence group left to right.

       ARG1 | ARG2
              ARG1 if it is neither null nor 0, otherwise ARG2

       ARG1 & ARG2
              ARG1 if neither argument is null or 0, otherwise 0

       ARG1 < ARG2,  ARG1 <= ARG2,  ARG1 = ARG2,
       ARG1 != ARG2, ARG1 >= ARG2,  ARG1 > ARG2
              1 if the comparison holds, 0 if not.  The comparison is
              numeric when BOTH arguments are integers and lexical
              otherwise, so `expr 10 '>' 9` is 1 but `expr 10 '>' 9a`
              compares the strings and is 0.

       ARG1 + ARG2,  ARG1 - ARG2
              arithmetic sum and difference

       ARG1 * ARG2,  ARG1 / ARG2,  ARG1 % ARG2
              arithmetic product, quotient and remainder.  Division by zero
              is an error, not zero.

       STRING : REGEXP
              anchored pattern match; see PATTERN MATCHING below

       match STRING REGEXP
              the same as STRING : REGEXP, written as a function

       substr STRING POSITION LENGTH
              the substring of STRING of at most LENGTH characters starting
              at POSITION, counting from 1.  A position or length outside the
              string yields the empty string rather than an error.

       index STRING CHARS
              the 1-based position in STRING of the first character that
              appears anywhere in CHARS, or 0 if none does

       length STRING
              the number of characters in STRING

       + TOKEN
              TOKEN as a string, even when it is also an operator name.  This
              is the only way to compare against a literal "match", "index",
              "substr" or "length".

       ( EXPRESSION )
              grouping

PATTERN MATCHING
       The : operator matches REGEXP -- a basic regular expression -- against
       STRING, anchored at the start of STRING whether or not the pattern
       begins with ^.

       What it yields depends on whether the pattern contains a \( \) group:

       o      with a group, the text that group matched, or the empty string
              if the match failed

       o      without a group, the NUMBER of characters matched, or 0 if the
              match failed

       The two failure values differ, which is what lets a caller tell "the
       pattern did not match" from "the group matched nothing".

EXIT STATUS
       0      EXPRESSION is neither null nor 0

       1      EXPRESSION is null or 0

       2      EXPRESSION is syntactically invalid

       3      an error occurred

EXAMPLES
       Arithmetic -- note the quoting of *:

              expr 6 '*' 7                       ->  42
              expr '(' 1 + 2 ')' '*' 3           ->  9

       Test whether a terminal name is a virtual console.  This is the exact
       idiom startx(1) uses, and it relies on the exit status rather than the
       output:

              if expr "$t" : '/dev/tty[0-9][0-9]*$' > /dev/null; then
                      echo "virtual console"
              fi

       Extract part of a string with a group:

              expr /usr/local/bin : '/usr/\(.*\)'   ->  local/bin

       Count characters matched, with no group:

              expr abcdef : 'abc'                ->  3

       String functions:

              expr length abcdef                 ->  6
              expr substr abcdef 2 3             ->  bcd
              expr index abcdef cd               ->  3

NOTES
       Neither | nor & short-circuits: both sides are always evaluated, so a
       syntax error on the right is reported even when the left already
       decides the result.

       An argument is treated as a number only when the whole of it is an
       integer.  "12abc" is a string; "  12  " is the number 12.

SEE ALSO
       test(1), tty(1), sed(1)
